.TH FLOOR 3M  "August 1, 1992"
.UC 4
.SH NAME
fabs, floor, ceil\- absolute value, floor, and ceiling functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.B double floor(double \fIx\fP);
.PP
.B double ceil(double \fIx\fP);
.PP
.B double fabs(double \fIx\fP);
.fi
.SH (ALSO AVAILABLE IN BSD)
.nf
.B double rint(double \fIx\fP);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fabs
returns the absolute value |\|\fIx\fP\||\|.
.PP
.I Floor
returns the largest integer no greater than \fIx\fP.
.PP
.I Ceil
returns the smallest integer no less than \fIx\fP.
.PP
.I Rint
returns the integer (represented as a double precision number)
nearest \fIx\fP in the direction of the prevailing rounding mode.
.SH NOTES
In the default rounding mode, to nearest,
on a machine that conforms to IEEE 754,
.IR rint (\fIx\fP)
is the integer nearest
.I x
with the additional stipulation
that if
|\|\fIrint\fP(\fIx\fP)\-\fIx\fP\||\|\|=\|1/2
then \fIrint\fP(\fIx\fP) is even.
Other rounding modes can make rint act like floor, or like ceil,
or round towards zero.
.PP
Another way to obtain an integer near \fIx\fP is to declare (in C):
.RS
double x;\0\0\0\0 int k;\0\0\0\0k\0=\0x;
.RE
Most C compilers round \fIx\fP towards 0 to get the integer \fIk\fP, but
some do otherwise.
If in doubt, use
.IR floor ,
.IR ceil ,
or
.I rint
first, whichever you intend.
Also note that, if \fIx\fP is larger than \fIk\fP can accommodate,
the value of
.I k
and the presence or absence of an integer overflow are hard to
predict.
.SH SEE ALSO
abs(3),
ieee(3M),
math(3M)
